SCHEMA location_schema '{iso standard 10303 part(41) version(4) object(1) location_schema(14)}';
  REFERENCE FROM support_resource_schema (identifier, label, text, bag_to_set);
  ENTITY location;
    id : identifier;
    name : label;
    description : OPTIONAL text;
  END_ENTITY;

  ENTITY location_relationship;
    id : identifier;
    name : label;
    description : OPTIONAL text;
    relating_location : location;
    related_location : location;
  END_ENTITY;

  FUNCTION acyclic_location_relationship (relation : location_relationship; relatives : SET OF location; specific_relation : STRING):BOOLEAN;
    LOCAL
      x : SET OF location_relationship;
    END_LOCAL;

    IF relation.relating_location IN relatives THEN
      RETURN (FALSE);
    END_IF;
    x := QUERY(lctn <* bag_to_set(USEDIN(relation.relating_location, 'LOCATION_SCHEMA.' + 'LOCATION_RELATIONSHIP.' + 'RELATED_LOCATION')) | specific_relation IN TYPEOF(lctn));
    REPEAT i := 1 TO HIINDEX(x);
      IF NOT acyclic_location_relationship(x[i], relatives + relation.relating_location, specific_relation) THEN
        RETURN (FALSE);
      END_IF;
    END_REPEAT;
    RETURN (TRUE);
  END_FUNCTION;

END_SCHEMA;

(*"location_schema"
== Introduction

The subject of the *location\_schema* is the identification and characterization of different representations of places or positions in space at which something can take place or exist. 

[example]
====
The identification of a geographical location is an example of generic data relating to a location.
====

== Fundamental concerns

This schema provides resources for the identification, naming, and description of:

* specific locations;
* relationships between specific locations.

expg_image:location_schemaexpg1.xml[]

expg_image:location_schemaexpg2.xml[]
*)

(*"location_schema.location"
A *location* is a place or position in space at which something can take place or exist. 

[NOTE]
--
The specification or representation of a location might take the form of a postal address, a set of geographical or spacial coordinates, or an association with a product.
--

[example]
====
A map reference specifies a location where a building exists.
====

[example]
====
The "bridge" of a particular ship specifies a location where navigation equipment should be installed.
====
*)

(*"location_schema.location.id"
the express_ref:[support_resource_schema.identifier] that distinguishes the *location*.
*)

(*"location_schema.location.name"
the express_ref:[support_resource_schema.label] by which the *location* is known.
*)

(*"location_schema.location.description"
the express_ref:[support_resource_schema.text] that characterizes the *location*. The value of this attribute need not be specified.
*)

(*"location_schema.location_relationship"
A *location\_relationship* is a relationship between two instances of the express_ref:[location_schema.location] entity data type and provides for the identification, naming, and description of that relationship.
*)

(*"location_schema.location_relationship.id"
the express_ref:[support_resource_schema.identifier] that distinguishes the *location\_relationship*.
*)

(*"location_schema.location_relationship.name"
the express_ref:[support_resource_schema.label] by which the *location\_relationship* is known.
*)

(*"location_schema.location_relationship.description"
the express_ref:[support_resource_schema.text] that characterizes the *location\_relationship*. The value of this attribute need not be specified.
*)

(*"location_schema.location_relationship.related_location"
the other instance of express_ref:[location_schema.location] that is a part of the relationship. If one element of the relationship is dependent upon the other, attribute shall be the dependent one.

[NOTE]
--
The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize this entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.
--
*)

(*"location_schema.location_relationship.relating_location"
one of the instances of express_ref:[location_schema.location] that is a part of the relationship.

[NOTE]
--
The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize this entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.
--
*)

(*"location_schema.acyclic_location_relationship"
The *acyclic\_location\_relationship* function determines whether the graph of instances of the entity data type express_ref:[location_schema.location] that contains relation as one of its links contains a cycle. This function may be used to evaluate either a express_ref:[location_schema.location_relationship] or any of its subtypes. 

The function returns TRUE if no cycle has been detected. Otherwise it returns FALSE.

[NOTE]
--
The algorithm of the function is explained in annex E.2.
--

[NOTE]
--
This function is not used in this schema. It is defined here because other ISO 10303 integrated resources and application protocols that use the express_ref:[location_schema.location_relationship] entity include rules that use this function.
--
*)

(*"location_schema.acyclic_location_relationship.relation"
(input) the candidate express_ref:[location_schema.location_relationship] to be checked.
*)

(*"location_schema.acyclic_location_relationship.relatives"
(input) the set of instances of the entity data type express_ref:[location_schema.location] that the function is searching for in the express_ref:[location_schema.location_relationship.relating_location] parameter of the *relation* argument.
*)

(*"location_schema.acyclic_location_relationship.specific_relation"
(input) the fully qualified name of a subtype of the express_ref:[location_schema.location_relationship] entity.
*)
